<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hmannan Yazawin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1832">1832</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Glass Palace Chronicle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Hmannan Yazawin, or Glass Palace Chronicle, is the official chronicle of the Burmese monarchy, compiled by a committee of scholars appointed by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagyidaw">Bagyidaw</a> in 1829 and completed in 1832. Drawing on earlier chronicles and inscriptions, it narrates the history of the Burmese kings from legendary origins to 1821, and it remained the standard account of Burmese history into the twentieth century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hmannan-yazawin.jpg" alt="Hmannan Yazawin" width="320" height="546" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Hybernator · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Glass_Palace_Chronicle.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The chronicle takes its name from the Hmannan, the Palace of Mirrors in the Inwa Palace complex where it was written, and from yazawin, from the Pali rājavaṃsa, chronicle of kings. King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bagyidaw">Bagyidaw</a> ordered the work in early 1829, soon after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-anglo-burmese-war">First Anglo-Burmese War</a> of 1824 to 1826, which had cost Konbaung Burma Arakan, Manipur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanintharyi-region">Tenasserim</a> coast and burdened the treasury with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-reparations">reparations</a> of one million pounds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pound-sterling">sterling</a>. The standard chronicle until then was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-yazawin">Maha Yazawin</a>, which reached 1711; the Yazawin Thit, commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodawpaya">Bodawpaya</a>, had been refused official status for its harsh criticism of earlier chronicles.</p><p>The 13 members of the Royal Historical Commission, learned monks, court historians and court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a>, first met on 11 May 1829. They drew on more than 600 inscriptions collected between 1783 and 1793, earlier chronicles, pagoda histories, Pali religious chronicles and Burmese poetry. The monk Monywe Zetawun Sayadaw led the work with Thawkabin Sayadaw, and the former monk and minister Maha Dhamma Thingyan decided which sources to accept.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The work took three years and four months and falls into two parts: from the earliest times to 1752, and the ruling Konbaung dynasty to 1821. For the dates of the Pagan kings it mostly kept those of the Yazawin Thit, though the Zatadawbon Yazawin later proved the most accurate.</p><p>The compilers left much of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maha-yazawin">Maha Yazawin</a> unchanged, and their commentaries are mostly esoteric. Their main change was religious: in place of the old story that the monarchy descended from Pyusawhti, son of a solar spirit and a dragon princess, they traced it to the Sakya clan of the Buddha through Abhiyaza of Tagaung. Monywe Sayadaw disagreed with parts of the result and issued his own Maha Yazawin Kyaw in 1831.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>British colonial scholars dismissed much of the chronicle's early history as copies of Indian legends, a view that prevailed at least to the 1960s, though prominent Burmese historians disagreed. Later research finds that, once the legends, now read as allegories, are set aside, its narratives largely fit the evidence, and its chronology after the 11th century is held reliable. Thai historians used it to correct the pre-1767 chronology of Siamese chronicles. Pe Maung Tin and Gordon Luce translated the part to the end of the Pagan dynasty into English as the Glass Palace Chronicle; the whole has been translated into Chinese.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hmannan Yazawin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Culturally, the period produced the <b>Hmannan Yazawin</b> (Glass Palace Chronicle, 1832), legal compilations, court drama and poetry, and important Buddhist scholarship, including the Fifth Buddhist Council convened by Mindon in 1871.</blockquote><a href="/empire/konbaung-dynasty">Konbaung dynasty · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The kingdom’s relative peace in the seventeenth century allowed a flowering of Theravada scholarship, monastic reform debates and Burmese-language literature, including legal texts and U Kala’s chronicle, on which later works such as the Konbaung <b>Hmannan Yazawin</b> drew.</blockquote><a href="/empire/restored-toungoo-kingdom">Restored Toungoo Kingdom · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
